  "answer": "No. The Aussie dollar is strong against the yen right now, although it has slipped a little from recent highs.\n\n**Latest rate:** A$1 bought **¥109.67** on 16 October 2026 (RBA 4pm rate, the most recent data).\n\n**Compared with the last few years:**\n- **Up about 20% from a year and a half ago:** it was ¥91.43 at the end of April 2025, and ¥89.10 in March 2023, the lowest month-end since January 2023.\n- **Up about 9% on a year ago:** it was ¥100.84 at the end of October 2025.\n- **Higher than its 2024 peak:** the highest month-end that year was ¥106.61 in June 2024.\n\n**Compared with the recent peak:**\n- It's about **4% below** the high of ¥114.54–114.63 in late August and early September 2026.\n- Most of that drop came in early September. Since then it has stayed in a narrow band of about ¥109.4–110.7 through October.\n\n**Longer view:** The data I have only goes back to 2023. From memory rather than this data, the A$ mostly bought about ¥75–85 between the mid-2010s and 2021, and peaked at around ¥105–107 in 2007 and 2013. If that's right, today's level of about ¥110 is high by historical standards too.\n\nSo the dollar has softened slightly over the past six weeks, but it isn't weak. If you're buying yen, for example for a trip to Japan, you're still getting one of the best rates in years.",
